//cloud computing 
         ~ grid computing + utility computing 
   Grid Computing : : Combine many computers (nodes) to solve large, complex tasks by dividing them into smaller subtasks. 
           Goal: Maximize computational power.
          Control: Usually distributed across participating nodes.
    Utiilty Computing : Main Idea: Provide computing as a measured service, just like electricity or water
          Goal: Pay-as-you-go — users pay only for what they use.
           Focus: Cost-efficiency, on-demand usage.
characteristics of cc :
    1. Outsource utilities (storage, computation, etc.)
        . You don’t need your own servers or infrastructure.
        . Instead, you use a Cloud Service Provider (CSP) like AWS, Azure, or Google Cloud.
        . These providers offer services like computing power, storage, and databases.
    2. Managed by the service provider
        . The cloud provider handles everything:
          server maintenance,security,backups,software updates 
        . You focus only on using the services, not managing them.
    3. Illusion of unlimited resources :
        . Even though physical resources are limited, cloud gives the feeling of infinite scalability.
        . You can scale up/down automatically based on your need.
        . This is possible due to virtualization and resource pooli
    4. Pay-as-you-go model
        . You only pay for what you use — compute hours, storage space, data transfer, etc.
        . This follows the utility computing model (like electricity billing).
    5. Accessible through a network :
        . You can access cloud services anywhere through the internet.
        . No need to be physically close to the data center.
        . Enables global access and remote work.
Cloud computing allows users to outsource infrastructure and services to third-party providers, giving the illusion of infinite resources, while being accessible online, managed for you, and billed based on usage.

