    For each device (or group of devices), you place a local computing unit nearby — called an edge device — to handle processing right there, without needing to send data to the cloud for every task.
    edge computing - it deos not rely on the cloud for every decision 
 pros :
    low latency 
    speedup
    more bandwidth 
    privacy 
 cons :
    lower computational power 
    lack of a global view 
    Edge devices have lower computational power because they are small, power-efficient units placed near the data source. 
    They are designed for fast, local tasks, not heavy processing like the cloud.
        | Limitation                 | Why It Happens in Edge                 |
    | -------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
    | ❌ No global awareness      | No central coordination or shared info |
    | 🧠 Low computational power | Limited hardware at the edge           |
    | 🔄 Hard to update          | No centralized software control        |
    | 📊 Data not unified        | Data stays local, not aggregated       |
    solution : 
    A hybrid approach — edge computing for real-time tasks, and cloud or fog for centralized coordination, updates, and analytics.

//Fog Computing 
   Fog computing sits between the edge devices (like sensors, mobile devices, or robots) and the central cloud.
   middle layer help with 
     Coordination
     Intermediate processing
     Data filtering
     Communication with the cloud
     Location-awareness
    1. “Move computation to a location between the nodes and the cloud”
      Fog servers are placed closer to the edge, often in local networks (e.g., at a factory, hospital, or campus).
      They do initial processing, filtering, and temporary storage before sending data to the cloud.
    “Fog servers can periodically connect with cloud servers”
       Yes — fog servers sync with cloud servers for backups and updates 
    Cache location-specific information”
       Fog servers store data relevant to a particular location (e.g., a museum floor, traffic zone, shopping mall).
    “Information from edge devices can be processed by fog servers”
      small real time decisions will be taken by cloud 
  
  Fog servers are like local assistants to the cloud — they are closer to the edge and can pre-process data, handle location-specific tasks, and still communicate with the cloud when needed.

    | Task Type                          | Who Handles It          | Why?                                   |
    | ---------------------------------- | ----------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
    | ⚡ **Real-time tasks**              | **Fog** (near the edge) | Fast, low latency response needed      |
    | 📉 **Filtering data**              | **Fog**                 | Reduce unnecessary data sent to cloud  |
    | 📍 **Location-specific decisions** | **Fog**                 | Fog servers are aware of local context |
    | 🧠 **Heavy computation**           | **Cloud**               | More powerful, can scale easily        |
    | 🗃️ **Long-term storage**          | **Cloud**               | Centralized and scalable storage       |
    | 📊 **Big data analytics**          | **Cloud**               | Requires large compute power           |
    | 🧬 **Machine learning training**   | **Cloud**               | Needs GPU clusters, huge memory        |

