//Mist computing 
   Mist computing is a very lightweight form of fog computing.
   It happens right at the edge of the network fabric — basically on the devices themselves or very close to them
   Uses microcomputers and microcontrollers (like Raspberry Pi, Arduino) to:
       Process some data locally
       Feed results to fog nodes or the cloud
    Key point: Mist is smaller, simpler, and closer to the actual devices than fog computing.

    Mist → Edge → Fog → Cloud
    Mist is lowest-level local processing, feeding into the bigger fog nodes.

    [SENSOR]
   |
   | (Mist computing happens here – tiny MCU)
   |
[EDGE DEVICE]
   |
   | (bigger processing + some storage)
   |
[FOG NODE]
   |
   | (regional server)
   |
[CLOUD]


//Dew computing 
   Dew computing is used when network connectivity is lost.
   A version of cloud functionality is replicated locally on your system (laptop, desktop, mobile).
   The system can still function offline, and syncs with the cloud later when the network is available
   Dew computing = offline-capable cloud


        | Layer     | Description                                  |
    | --------- | -------------------------------------------- |
    | **Mist**  | Tiny processing at the device level          |
    | **Edge**  | Devices themselves acting as servers         |
    | **Fog**   | Local servers that aggregate edge/mist data  |
    | **Cloud** | Centralized powerful computation and storage |
    | **Dew**   | Local “mini-cloud” to function offline       |
    

    | Feature              | Dew Computing                 | Cloud Computing           |
    | -------------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------- |
    | **Connectivity**     | Works offline (local copy)    | Requires internet         |
    | **Storage**          | Local storage with sync       | Centralized cloud storage |
    | **User Interaction** | Direct human interaction      | Mostly remote access      |
    | **Processing**       | Local (limited)               | Centralized, powerful     |
    | **Example**          | Dropbox, Google Drive offline | AWS, Google Cloud         |


| **Layer** | **Definition**                                                                   | **Example**                                                                     |
| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Mist**  | Tiny processing done **inside the sensor or device** itself with minimal power.  | A **smart sensor** that turns on a light when it detects motion.                |
| **Edge**  | Processing done **on/near the device** using smart gateways or powerful devices. | A **smart camera** that detects faces or motion without sending data to cloud.  |
| **Fog**   | **Local servers/gateways** that collect and preprocess data from edge/mist.      | A **factory server** that analyzes data from many machines before cloud upload. |
| **Cloud** | **Centralized data centers** with powerful computing and storage.                | **Google Cloud** analyzing smart home data or running machine learning models.  |
| **Dew**   | **Offline-first local systems** that sync with cloud when connected.             | A **remote health device** storing patient data offline and syncing later.      |



